---

# MEASURED LEAK CHANNELS LINKING MULTIPLE PSEUDONYMS ON ONE DEVICE / DEVICE FLEET

**Method note:** OpenAlex budget was exhausted at session start; arXiv's API returns empty via `curl` but works via WebFetch. Reuters, AP, Wired, Verge, Washington Post, and Google Support (CAPTCHA) are all unreachable from this egress — noted inline as NOT REACHED with substitutes where found.

---

## 1. PUSH NOTIFICATION METADATA — the strongest measured channel

### 1a. The Wyden letter (PRIMARY SOURCE, full text retrieved)

**December 6, 2023**, Sen. Ron Wyden to AG Merrick Garland.
URL: https://www.wyden.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/wyden_smartphone_push_notification_surveillance_letter.pdf
Press release: https://www.wyden.senate.gov/news/press-releases/wyden-seeks-answers-from-justice-department-on-alleged-surveillance-of-apple-and-google-mobile-push-notifications

Verbatim, the load-bearing paragraph:

> "The data these two companies receive includes metadata, detailing **which app received a notification and when, as well as the phone and associated Apple or Google account to which that notification was intended to be delivered**. In certain instances, they also might also receive unencrypted content..."

Timeline in Wyden's own words: tip received **spring 2022**; ~one year of staff investigation; both companies said the information "is restricted from public release by the government." He asks DOJ to permit aggregate statistics and customer notification.

**This is the exact linkage primitive for your threat model.** The resolution direction is `push token → the ONE account/device`, not `push token → the app persona`. Per-app token separation (below) buys nothing against it.

### 1b. Token scope — OFFICIAL DOCS (retrieved via Apple's docs JSON API)

**APNs** — https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/registering-your-app-with-apns

> "This address takes the form of a device token **unique to both the device and your app**."
> "**You can't use the same device token for more than one app, even when the apps are on the same device.** Every app must request their own unique device token."

Note the double edge: the token is app-scoped (good for app-vs-app isolation), but APNs itself holds the device↔account binding, so the *server side* re-joins what the token space separated.

Also: "APNs issues a new token when the user restores a device from a backup, when the user installs your app on a new device, and when the user reinstalls the operating system."

**FCM** — https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/manage-tokens
Registration token is scoped to **an app instance on a device**, derived from the Firebase Installation ID (FID). Expires after **270 days** of inactivity, then re-registers, "Note that the FID may change."

### 1c. Apple's response and the measured request volume (STRONGEST NUMBERS IN THIS REPORT)

Apple's verbatim statement, Dec 2023 (via Techdirt, https://www.techdirt.com/2023/12/11/letter-from-sen-wyden-to-the-doj-says-governments-are-gathering-push-notification-data-from-google-apple/):

> "In this case, the federal government prohibited us from sharing any information. Now that this method has become public we are updating our transparency reporting to detail these kinds of requests."

Google's: it shares Wyden's "commitment to keeping users informed about these requests." (Reuters/AP/Verge originals NOT REACHED; Axios 403.)

Apple **did** follow through. There is now a standing "Push Token Requests" category:
- https://www.apple.com/legal/transparency/push-token.html
- Machine-readable: https://www.apple.com/legal/zip/transparency/Apple_Transparency_Report.zip → `push_token_requests.csv`

Apple's own definition: requests "based on an Apple Push Notification service token identifier," which "generally seek **identifying details of the Apple Account associated with the device's push token**, such as name, physical address and email address." Apple notes it "was only permitted to disclose information about Push Token requests starting in Transparency Report Period July 1 – December 31, 2022."

**Measured global totals (from the CSV, aggregated):**

| Period | Requests | **Push tokens specified** | Requests where data provided |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2022 H2 | 70 | 794 | 54 |
| 2023 H1 | 119 | 234 | 90 |
| 2023 H2 | 232 | 1,180 | 181 |
| 2024 H1 | 277 | 545 | 164 |
| 2024 H2 | 312 | 743 | 191 |
| 2025 H1 | 393 | 966 | 278 |
| 2025 H2 | 489 | 867 | 332 |

Requests grew **7.0×** from 2022 H2 to 2025 H2. Note tokens-specified routinely exceeds requests by 2–5×; some single requests are bulk (Netherlands 2022 H2: **2 requests / 687 tokens**; Israel 2023 H2: **1 request / 694 tokens**; US 2025 H1: 168 requests / **718 tokens**).

**Fifteen requesting countries appear across the series**, answering "which governments": United States, United Kingdom, Germany, Netherlands, Singapore, **France** (2023 H2: 1 req; 2025 H1: 3 req/5 tokens), Israel, Canada, Poland, Australia, Denmark, Finland, India, Moldova, Norway, Sweden, Switzerland, Belgium.

The UK is the volume outlier and rising fast: 27 → 60 → 123 → 141 → 153 → 206 → **290** requests, at an 80–90% fulfilment rate throughout. The US fulfils far less (28–54% in 2024–25) but specifies the most tokens.

**EU/France angle:** France appears in the data twice, but I found **no** separate EU regulatory action on push metadata. NOT REACHED / likely does not exist as a distinct proceeding.

**Google's equivalent:** Google's transparency report was NOT REACHED (google.com/sorry CAPTCHA on two attempts). I found no evidence Google publishes a push/FCM-specific request category. Treat as **unconfirmed, likely absent** — Apple is the only one of the two with a measured public series.

**Wyden follow-ups (2024/2025):** I enumerated wyden.senate.gov's search index across three pages for "push notification surveillance." **Only the Dec 2023 letter and its press release exist on the site.** No 2024 or 2025 follow-up letter. Negative finding.

### For your architecture
This is the channel that most directly defeats data-layer persona separation. A device holding N pseudonymous personas presents N push tokens; every one resolves, at Apple/Google, to the same account and the same handset. The join happens in a jurisdiction you don't control, is legally compelled, is growing ~7× over three years, and — per Apple's own category definition — the *purpose* of the request is precisely to perform that resolution.

---

## 2. DEVICE ATTESTATION AS A LINKAGE VECTOR

### 2a. Apple DeviceCheck — two bits, per device, explicitly (OFFICIAL DOCS)

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/devicecheck

> "you can get a token that you use on your server to **set and query two binary digits of data per device**, while maintaining user privacy."

Query/update API: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/devicecheck/accessing-and-modifying-per-device-data — fields `bit0`, `bit1`, plus `last_update_time` returned as **YYYY-MM** (deliberately coarsened). Auth JWT is created "the same steps you use to create a provider authentication token for APNs."

### 2b. Apple App Attest — per app, per device, **and Apple recommends per-account**

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/devicecheck/establishing-your-app-s-integrity

> "**For each user account on each device running your app**, generate a unique, hardware-based, cryptographic key pair..."
> "**Don't reuse a key among multiple users on a device** because this weakens security protections."

This is a genuinely favourable finding: Apple's own guidance is per-account key derivation, which is persona-compatible. But the private key lives in the Secure Enclave and Apple attests to it, so the *attestation* still names the hardware. DeviceCheck's two bits remain strictly device-scoped and are the linkage vector; App Attest is not.

### 2c. Android Play Integrity — and `deviceRecall`, the sharpest finding in this section

https://developer.android.com/google/play/integrity/verdicts — verdict fields: `requestDetails`, `appIntegrity`, `deviceIntegrity`, `accountDetails`, `environmentDetails`.
- Device-bound: `deviceRecognitionVerdict` (`MEETS_DEVICE_INTEGRITY` / `MEETS_BASIC_INTEGRITY` / `MEETS_STRONG_INTEGRITY` / `MEETS_VIRTUAL_INTEGRITY`), `deviceAttributes.sdkVersion`, `recentDeviceActivity.deviceActivityLevel`, **`deviceRecall`**.
- Account-bound: `accountDetails.appLicensingVerdict` only (`LICENSED`/`UNLICENSED`/`UNEVALUATED`).

**Device Recall** — https://developer.android.com/google/play/integrity/device-recall (currently **beta**):

> "You can read **three custom values or bits for each device**... you could combine them to represent eight custom labels."
> "The data is **stored on Google's servers**, allowing your app to reliably recall your custom data **even after your app is reinstalled or the device is reset**."

Google's own guardrail is a *policy*, not a mechanism:

> "Device recall preserves user privacy because the requesting app can only recall the limited data that it associated with devices, without accessing any device or user identifiers."
> "**You may not use device recall to fingerprint or track individual users or devices**..."

This is Android's DeviceCheck, with one extra bit and **factory-reset survival**. Three bits of server-side, app-scoped, reset-surviving per-device state is a persistent handle across every persona that app ever hosts. The only thing standing between it and cross-persona linkage is Google's terms of service.

**SafetyNet Attestation** — https://developer.android.com/privacy-and-security/safetynet/deprecation-timeline:
> "The SafetyNet Attestation API was deprecated in **2022** and fully turned down in **January 2025**."
Calls now fail with `ApiException` status code 7 (`NETWORK_ERROR`). Migration path is Play Integrity.

### 2d. Web Environment Integrity — the backlash, and its own admission

Proposal: https://github.com/RupertBenWiser/Web-Environment-Integrity/blob/main/explainer.md
Header now reads: "⚠️ **NOTE: This proposal is no longer pursued.**"

The explainer concedes the vector in its own words:
> "attestation tokens themselves **could enable cross-site tracking if they are re-used between sites**."
Mitigations proposed: "top-level partitioning for all attester tokens," "All fields returned by attesters must be **low-entropy** so as not to be uniquely identifying," and explicit exclusion of "A device ID that is a unique identifier accessible to API consumers."

Measured backlash (HN Algolia, points as proxy):
- Vivaldi, 2023-07-26, 756 pts — https://vivaldi.com/blog/googles-new-dangerous-web-environment-integrity-spec/
- The proposal repo, 2023-07-21, 639 pts
- FSF, 2023-07-28, 415 pts — https://www.fsf.org/blogs/community/web-environment-integrity-is-an-all-out-attack-on-the-free-internet
- W3C TAG: "Web Environment Integrity has no standing at W3C," 2023-08-11 — https://www.w3.org/blog/2023/web-environment-integrity-has-no-standing-at-w3c/
- Abandonment, The Register, 2023-11-02 — https://www.theregister.com/2023/11/02/google_abandons_web_environment_integrity/
- Brave disabled it in-tree: https://github.com/brave/brave-core/pull/19476

**Academic attestation-privacy work:** the honest answer is that the anonymous-attestation literature is *cryptographic*, not empirical. Direct Anonymous Attestation (Brickell/Camenisch/Chen, and Enhanced Privacy ID — https://doi.org/10.1145/1314333.1314337, 2007) exists precisely to give user-controlled linkability, and there is a known-good fix paper (Camenisch et al., "One TPM to Bind Them All: Fixing TPM 2.0 for Provably Secure Anonymous Attestation," IEEE S&P 2017, https://doi.org/10.1109/sp.2017.22). But an arXiv search for `"remote attestation" AND privacy AND linkability` returns **0 results**. There is **no measured study of Play Integrity or App Attest as a fingerprinting channel**. That is a real gap — and it means the linkage risk here is *designed-in and documented*, not *measured*.

---

## 3. TIMING / TRAFFIC CORRELATION

### 3a. Website fingerprinting — the attack, the ceiling, and the critique

**Panchenko et al., "Website Fingerprinting at Internet Scale," NDSS 2016** — DOI 10.14722/ndss.2016.23477, PDF https://www.ndss-symposium.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/website-fingerprinting-internet-scale.pdf

Dataset: **over 300,000 webpages** — "ten times larger than the biggest set used before." Their headline is a *negative* result and it is the single most important sentence for a threat model:

> "for the first time we show that **no existing method – including our own – scales when applied in realistic settings**."
> "webpage fingerprinting does not scale for any considered page in our datasets and any state-of-the-art classifier. Hence, **the attack cannot be reliably used to convict users, but it may be used to limit the set of possible suspects**."

Background-set sizes swept b = 1,000 → 111,884; both precision and recall decline monotonically at every step. Crucially, **website** fingerprinting (a whole domain) scales far better than **webpage** fingerprinting: closed-world index-page separation of 20 sites hits **99% accuracy**, and in the open world "the precision stabilizes on a high level in the case of website classification."

**Sirinam et al., "Deep Fingerprinting," CCS 2018** — arXiv:1801.02265, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.02265
- Closed world (95 sites × 1,000 traces): **98.3%** accuracy undefended
- Against WTF-PAD: **90%**
- Against Walkie-Talkie: **49.7%** (near the theoretical max for W-T)
- Open world, 20,000 unmonitored sites, undefended: **0.99 precision / 0.94 recall**
- Open world against WTF-PAD: **0.95 precision / 0.70 recall** (abstract states 0.96/0.68; body states 0.95/0.70 — the paper is internally inconsistent by ~1–2 points)

**Juarez et al., "A Critical Evaluation of Website Fingerprinting Attacks," CCS 2014** — DOI 10.1145/2660267.2660368, PDF https://www.esat.kuleuven.be/cosic/publications/article-2456.pdf

The measured fragilities, and these are the ones that matter for a long-lived pseudonym:
- **Time decay: "it takes less than 10 days to drop under 50%"** accuracy, training at t=0 and testing across 90 days. They restricted all other experiments to a 5-day window because of it.
- **Multi-tab browsing:** with a 5-second gap between opening two tabs, accuracy "drops by over 68%" relative to control — even though ~30% of the trace remained uncovered by background traffic.
- Verification step reduces false positives "**over 63%**" but "does not completely solve the problem."
- Aggregate: "for some of the variables, **the accuracy can drop up to 70%**."

### 3b. End-to-end flow correlation — DeepCorr

**Nasr, Bahramali, Houmansadr, "DeepCorr," CCS 2018** — arXiv:1808.07285, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1808.07285

The requested numbers, verbatim from the paper:
- **"by collecting only about 900 packets of each target Tor flow (roughly 900KB of Tor data), DeepCorr provides a flow correlation accuracy of 96% compared to 4% by the state-of-the-art system of RAPTOR using the same exact setting."** A 24× gap.
- With only **300 packets**: TP ≈ **0.8** at FP = 10⁻³, "compared to less than 0.05 by prior work."
- With ℓ = **100 packets**: TP = **0.62** at FP = 10⁻³.
- Trained on **25,000 Tor flows**, ~1 day on a single TITAN X. "an adversary needs to re-train DeepCorr only about **once every month**" — note this is far more durable than website fingerprinting's 10-day half-life.
- Per-correlation cost **2 ms** vs RAPTOR's >20 ms at matched 95% accuracy.
- Pluggable transports: "**meek and obfs4-iat0 provide little protection**"; obfs4-iat1 is better but barely deployed.

### 3c. Onion-service and circuit fingerprinting

**Kwon, AlSabah, Lazar, Dacier, Devadas, "Circuit Fingerprinting Attacks," USENIX Security 2015** — https://www.usenix.org/system/files/conference/usenixsecurity15/sec15-paper-kwon.pdf

Passive, no active probing:
- Detect that a user is *using a hidden service at all*: **>98% TPR, <0.1% FPR** (attack 1); **99% TPR, 0.07% FPR** (attack 2). "This detection reduces the anonymity set of a user from millions of Tor users to just the users of hidden services."
- Then WF *within* that set: which of 50 monitored pages, **88% TPR / 2.9% FPR**; deanonymizing 50 monitored hidden-service **servers**, **88% TPR / 7.8% FPR**, open world.

Their framing directly rebuts Juarez: "we revisit their findings and demonstrate that **the world of hidden services is the ideal setting to wage WF attacks**." The reason is exactly your topic — a small, stable, long-lived set of endpoints defeats the base-rate and time-decay defences that protect the open web.

**Biryukov, Pustogarov, Weinmann, "Trawling for Tor Hidden Services," IEEE S&P 2013** — https://www.ieee-security.org/TC/SP2013/papers/4977a080.pdf

The uptime/longevity finding, with a price tag:
- Harvest **all** hidden-service descriptors — "a global picture of all hidden services in Tor" — in **~2 days**, 50 (later 58) EC2 instances, ~3% descriptor loss.
- Targeted deanonymization: a single EUR 45/month guard had **0.6%** selection probability; 23 such servers give **13.8%** per-try; **within 8 months, >90% probability** of becoming a long-running hidden service's guard, total **EUR 8,280 (~USD 11,000)** — "this scales well: Attacking multiple hidden services can be achieved for the same cost once the infrastructure is running."

**A long-lived pseudonymous service is priced in months of uptime, not in cryptographic strength.**

### 3d. Linking multiple pseudonyms by correlated activity timing — MOSTLY A GAP

I searched Crossref and arXiv specifically for this. The finding is largely negative and worth stating plainly:

The "user identity linkage" (UIL) literature is real but is **graph-, content-, and mobility-based, not device-timing-based**. Representative:
- Chen et al., "HFUL," arXiv:2201.02830 (2022) — spatio-temporal, but the signal is **GPS check-ins**, not network timing.
- "Correlation-Attention Masked Temporal Transformer for User Identity Linkage Using Heterogeneous Mobility Data," arXiv:2504.01979 (2025) — reports only relative gains (**12.92%–17.76% Macro-F1**, 5.80%–8.38% AUC improvement over baselines); no absolute linkage rate in the abstract.
- Survey: "User Identity Linkage on Social Networks: A Review of Modern Techniques and Applications," arXiv:2409.08966 (2024).
- Notably, most UIL abstracts (I checked 12) **report no absolute accuracy at all** — they report improvements over baselines. This literature is not calibrated for threat modelling.

**There is no measured study of "same device, N pseudonyms, linked by correlated activity timing" that I could find.** The closest measured thing is §5 below (Careless Whisper), which does it as an *active probe* rather than a passive correlation, and gets device-fleet enumeration for free.

---

## 4. IP / RELAY-LEVEL CORRELATION OF ACCOUNTS

**FTC, "Cross-Device Tracking: An FTC Staff Report," January 2017** — https://www.ftc.gov/reports/cross-device-tracking-federal-trade-commission-staff-report-january-2017 · PDF https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/documents/reports/cross-device-tracking-federal-trade-commission-staff-report-january-2017/ftc_cross-device_tracking_report_1-23-17.pdf

Quoting the mechanism directly:

> "A common method of probabilistic tracking is **IP address**... Because devices on the same local network often have the same public IP address, the ad platform might infer that a computer, smartphone, and tablet that use the same public IP address **belong to the same household**."
> "if a consumer's smartphone uses the same public IP address as her work computer during business hours, and then uses the same public IP address as her home computer during non-business hours, an ad platform might infer..."

The report notes IP address is "one of the most commonly used data points" for this, and that industry fuses the two approaches: "companies that have deterministic data... frequently work with entities engaged in probabilistic tracking"; one firm "leverages a small core of deterministic data... to **train its probabilistic device graph**."

The report is **qualitative on accuracy**. It gives no measured linkage rate. The numbers it does carry are consumer-behaviour ones (30–50% of users delete cookies monthly; 29% of US consumers used ad blockers as of Jan 2016; 419 million / 22% of world smartphone users blocking mobile web ads). **NOT MEASURED: cross-device match accuracy.** I found no public measurement of IP-based household-graph precision. This appears to be genuinely unmeasured in the open literature — the graphs are proprietary.

Kollnig et al. (below) supplies the mechanism-level confirmation from the app side: locale, device model, and OS version "can be used to **disambiguate different users connecting from the same IP address** (e.g. households sharing the same Wi-Fi router) – and even across different IP addresses through the use of additional, first-party data."

---

## 5. PRESENCE AND TYPING INDICATORS — the best on-topic measured result

### 5a. Careless Whisper (RAID 2025, **Best Paper**) — DIRECTLY ON YOUR THESIS

**Gegenhuber, Günther, Maier, Judmayer, Holzbauer, Frenzel, Ullrich (U. Vienna / SBA Research), "Careless Whisper: Exploiting Silent Delivery Receipts to Monitor Users on Mobile Instant Messengers."**
DOI 10.1109/raid67961.2025.00068 · arXiv:2411.11194v4 (v4 dated 2025-10-31) · https://arxiv.org/pdf/2411.11194v4

This paper is the single closest match to your topic in the entire literature, because it measures **device-fleet enumeration** as a side effect of presence.

Mechanism: crafted messages trigger **silent delivery receipts** — no notification on the target side, no existing conversation required, target addressable **by phone number alone**. Read receipts can be disabled; **delivery receipts cannot**, "due to design choices of the underlying protocol." Affected: **WhatsApp and Signal** (Threema also analysed).

Measured results:
- **Screen on/off leaks through RTT.** Screen-on RTTs ≤ 1 second; screen-off > 1 second (Fig. 1, iPhone/WhatsApp, 1 Hz sampling). Enables tracking a victim's screen time.
- **Device-fleet enumeration.** Probing a target's device list returned `[0, 1, 9]` — "three existing sessions, one main device (index 0), and two companion devices (index 1, 9). Due to the **(auto-incrementing) index, we know in which order the sessions were created**."
- **Per-device liveness and per-device OS.** In an open-world walk-to-the-office capture on a volunteer with **no prior relation to the attacker** ("the worst attack scenario of a spooky stranger"), they distinguished: desktop on LAN (stable RTT, low jitter) vs phone; the moment the desktop shut down at 19:28; the phone's Wi-Fi→LTE handover; a phone call (denser receipts); arrival at the office at 19:45 and LTE→Wi-Fi; a work laptop (device 9) waking and flushing missed receipts. From receipt stacking order: "we know that this device is a **macOS computer running the WhatsApp desktop client**."
- Resource exhaustion, same channel, no notification: **3.7 MB/s = 13.3 GB/hour** of forced traffic on WhatsApp from a single session; battery drain per hour iPhone 13 Pro **14%**, iPhone 11 **18%**, Samsung Galaxy S23 **15%**, against <1% idle. Signal's stricter rate limits held it to 1%/hour.
- Prior work (ref [21], same group) had already shown **country-level geolocation** from receipt RTT using ordinary messages; this paper's contribution is going silent and sub-second.

Responsible disclosure to platform operators; the authors "argue for a design change."

**Why this matters to you specifically:** the mechanism enumerates *how many sessions a person runs, in what creation order, on what OSes, and which are live right now* — from a phone number. Every persona sharing that fleet inherits the same session index space. This is the transport layer publishing your fleet topology while the data layer holds perfectly.

### 5b. The keystroke-timing lineage

**Song, Wagner, Tian, "Timing Analysis of Keystrokes and Timing Attacks on SSH," USENIX Security 2001** — https://www.usenix.org/legacy/events/sec01/full_papers/song/song.pdf

The two structural weaknesses, in the authors' words: packets "padded only to an eight-byte boundary... which reveals the approximate size of the original data," and "in interactive mode, **every individual keystroke that a user types is sent to the remote machine in a separate IP packet immediately after the key is pressed**."

Measured:
- Inter-keystroke latency yields **~1.2 bits of information per character pair**; for a password of length T, "approximately 1.2T bits."
- Their Herbivore attack "**reduces the cost of password cracking by a factor of 50**" — "Herbivore only needs to test 1/50 times as many" candidates; equivalently ~5.7 bits learned per password.
- Context: "the entropy of written English is very low (about **0.6–1.3** bits per character)."

The direct read-across: any typing-indicator that is emitted per-keystroke or at keystroke granularity reproduces exactly the SSH-interactive weakness at the application layer, and does so *across* whichever persona is typing.

### 5c. WhatsApp online-status monitoring — earlier, weaker

"Research: Large-Scale WhatsApp User Monitoring," 2014-12-09, https://www.onlinestatusmonitor.com/data_summary/ — this is the long-cited demonstration that online/last-seen status can be polled at scale to reconstruct sleep and activity patterns. **NOT REACHED** (I did not fetch the page; it surfaced only via HN Algolia). Treat as a pointer, not a citation. The 2025 RAID paper above supersedes it with proper measurement.

Platform response timeline (secondary, HN-surfaced): WhatsApp began rolling out "hide online status" on iOS around 2022-07 (https://9to5mac.com/2022/07/02/whatsapp-hide-online-status/) and broadly by 2022-11. Note this control covers *online status* and does **not** cover delivery receipts, which §5a shows cannot be disabled.

---

## 6. BACKUP METADATA

This section is the thinnest, and I want to be honest that most of what circulates here is claimed rather than measured.

**Measured (Apple's own transparency CSVs, same zip as §1c):**

| Period | Device requests | Devices specified | Account requests | Accounts specified |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2023 H1 | 28,396 | 159,010 | 18,646 | 56,692 |
| 2023 H2 | 27,691 | 133,282 | 20,405 | 58,549 |
| 2024 H1 | 40,905 | 556,410 | 25,496 | 72,254 |
| 2024 H2 | 34,563 | 464,666 | 28,745 | 82,729 |
| 2025 H1 | 28,825 | 662,705 | 29,825 | 81,781 |
| 2025 H2 | 29,948 | 536,317 | 32,240 | 95,241 |

Account requests — the category that reaches iCloud content including backups — rose steadily to **32,240 requests / 95,241 accounts** in 2025 H2. Devices-specified spiked ~4× in 2024 H1 and has stayed high. The zip also contains `us_national_security_requests.csv` and `uk_data_access_agreement_ipa_warrant_requests.csv`, both of which are relevant and which I did not aggregate.

**Design facts (via Wikipedia's iCloud article, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ICloud, with its own citations):**
- Standard iCloud Backup is **not** end-to-end encrypted; Apple holds the keys.
- The compounding defect: "if iCloud Backup is enabled, **the encryption key for Messages in iCloud is part of the backup**, allowing Apple to access users' entire iMessage history if served with a search warrant." An E2EE messaging layer is undone by the backup layer.
- Advanced Data Protection: announced **2022-12-07**, US **2022-12-13**, worldwide **2023-01-23**; optional, opt-in.
- **February 2025:** UK government pressure led Apple to disable ADP for new users in that jurisdiction. Note the alignment with §1c — the UK is also by far the largest push-token requester.

**Google Android backup E2EE: NOT REACHED.** support.google.com CAPTCHA'd on two attempts, and the security blog post body did not render. Google has publicly described screen-lock-derived, Titan-HSM-protected backup encryption, but **I could not retrieve the primary text and will not quote it from memory.**

**No measured study of backup metadata as a cross-persona linkage vector was found.** This is a genuine gap. The mechanism is obvious — one backup contains every persona's app state, and one account holds the backup — but nobody has measured it.

---

## 7. BROWSER / DEVICE FINGERPRINTING ACROSS PERSONAS

**Eckersley, "How Unique Is Your Web Browser?" (Panopticlick), PETS 2010** — https://coveryourtracks.eff.org/static/browser-uniqueness.pdf
- **470,161 browsers** sampled.
- Fingerprint distribution carries **at least 18.1 bits of entropy** — "only one in **286,777** other browsers will share its fingerprint."
- With Flash or Java: **18.8 bits**; **94.2%** of such browsers were unique in the sample.
- On *re-linking across change* — the persona-relevant part: fingerprints changed rapidly, "but even a simple heuristic was usually able to guess when a fingerprint was an 'upgraded' version of a previously observed browser's fingerprint, with **99.1% of guesses correct and a false positive rate of only 0.86%**."
- The self-defeat warning, still true: "anti-fingerprinting privacy technologies can be **self-defeating if they are not used by a sufficient number of people**."

**Laperdrix, Rudametkin, Baudry, "Beauty and the Beast," IEEE S&P 2016** — DOI 10.1109/sp.2016.57, PDF https://www.ieee-security.org/TC/SP2016/papers/0824a878.pdf
- **118,934 fingerprints**, **17 attributes** (AmIUnique dataset).
- **90% of desktop** fingerprints uniquely identified; **81% of mobile** (13,105 mobile fingerprints), "despite the lack of plugins and fonts" — mobile is carried instead by rich user-agents and canvas.
- Canvas is "one of the most discriminating attributes"; **89.4%** of browsers uniquely identifiable with it in play (their comparison figure); **95%** uniquely identified among the Flash-bearing subset.
- Simulated defence: removing plugins and standardising HTTP headers "could reduce desktop fingerprint's uniqueness by **a very strong 36%**."

**Cao, Li, Wijmans, "(Cross-)Browser Fingerprinting via OS and Hardware Level Features," NDSS 2017** — DOI 10.14722/ndss.2017.23152, PDF https://yinzhicao.org/TrackingFree/crossbrowsertracking_NDSS17.pdf

The requested figure, confirmed at source:
- **Single-browser: 99.24%** of users identified, "as opposed to **90.84%** for AmIUnique, i.e., state of the art, on the same dataset."
- **Cross-browser: 83.24% uniqueness with 91.44% cross-browser stability**, versus prior work (Boda et al., excluding IP address) at **68.98% uniqueness / 84.64% stability**.
- IE and Edge on the same machine: **100% uniqueness with 95.7% cross-browser stability** — "they probably share a" common implementation.
- Method: OS/hardware-level probes (GPU rendering via WebGL, CPU virtual cores, installed writing scripts/fonts, screen attributes) — chosen precisely because they survive the browser boundary.
- The authors' own framing: this is a "**2.5-generation**" technique, between second-gen (stateless, single-browser) and third-gen (cross-device).

**Vastel, Laperdrix, Rudametkin, Rouvoy, "FP-STALKER: Tracking Browser Fingerprint Evolutions," IEEE S&P 2018** — DOI 10.1109/sp.2018.00008. **PAPER PDF NOT REACHED** — HAL is behind Anubis anti-bot, IEEE stamp-PDF and the author's site both returned HTML. From the IEEE S&P 2018 program page (https://www.ieee-security.org/TC/SP2018/program.html, a **weak secondary source**, flagged as such): ~**100,000 fingerprints** from ~**1,900 browser instances**, average tracking duration ~**54 days**, with **over a quarter of browsers tracked beyond 100 days**. Verify these against the paper before relying on them.

**Read together, §7 says:** two personas in two different *browsers* on one machine are linkable at 83.24% (Cao), and a single persona's fingerprint drift is re-linkable at 99.1% correct / 0.86% FP (Eckersley). Browser separation is not persona separation.

---

## 8. MOBILE ADVERTISING ID — CROSS-APP LINKAGE, 2021–2022

**Kollnig, Shuba, Binns, Van Kleek, Shadbolt, "Are iPhones Really Better for Privacy?" PETS 2022** — arXiv:2109.13722
- **24,000 apps** (Android + iOS), 2020 snapshot.
- The AdId "is **the only cross-app identifier** that may be used for advertising on Android."
- Measured sharing over the network: AdId shared by **55.4% of Android** apps and **31.0% of iOS** apps.
- **85.1% of Android and 61.4% of iOS** apps shared device model and phone name — "which can be used as part of device fingerprinting."
- Other Android identifiers still observed in traffic: Android ID **18.2%**, IMEI **1.3%**, serial **1.1%**, WiFi MAC **0.6%** (all now blocked as of Android 10).
- On iOS, cross-app identifier access was near-universal among ad SDKs: among apps with Unity3d Ads, **>99%** accessed the AdID; Moat **100%**; Inmobi **>94%**.

**Kollnig, Shuba, Van Kleek, Binns, Shadbolt, "Goodbye Tracking? Impact of iOS App Tracking Transparency and Privacy Labels," FAccT 2022** — DOI 10.1145/3531146.3533116, arXiv:2204.03556
- **1,759 iOS apps** compared before vs after ATT (UK App Store).
- ATT works *as designed*: "Apple's new policies, as promised, **prevent the collection of the IDFA**." Only **24.7%** of post-ATT apps can access the IDFA on iOS ≥14.5. Between **60% and 95%** of users refuse tracking when asked.
- **But the tracking did not go away:** "the number of tracking libraries has – on average – roughly stayed the same." Mean tracking libraries 3.7 before → 3.6 after; **86.39%** of apps still contained at least one. Average tracking domains contacted *before any user interaction* went **up**: 4.0 before → 4.7 after.
- **The finding that matters most here — measured cross-app re-identification rebuilt server-side:**
  > "we found **9 apps that were able to generate a mutual user identifier that can be used for cross-app tracking, through the use of server-side code**. These 9 apps used an '**AAID**'... implemented and generated by **Umeng, a subsidiary of the Chinese tech company Alibaba**."
  This "**violat[es] Apple's policies**."
- And the general mechanism, stated plainly:
  > "large tracking companies can likely still use these identifiers to build profiles of an app user's journey across apps, **using their server-side code to link different identifiers together** (e.g. through the user's IP address, other device information, and first-party data)."
- Device information still shared post-ATT and usable "for fingerprinting or cohort tracking" — device model **74.5%** after (up from 60.2%), locale **90.1%**, carrier, timezone, ARM/memory string.
- Also: Google "assigned an `android_id` to an iOS app upon first contact."

**This is the empirical proof of the general shape:** kill the platform-blessed cross-app identifier and the ecosystem reconstitutes one out of device attributes plus IP plus server-side joins, within a year, in shipping apps.

---

## SYNTHESIS — what is MEASURED vs what is only CLAIMED

**Measured, hard, and directly against multi-persona-on-one-device:**
1. **Push token → account resolution.** Apple's own numbers: 489 requests / 867 tokens in 2025 H2 alone, 15 countries, 7× growth since 2022 H2, ~68% fulfilment. Apple's own category definition says the point is to get the *account* behind the token. Per-app token scoping does not help.
2. **Play Integrity `deviceRecall`.** 3 bits, Google-server-side, **survives app reinstall and factory reset**. Restrained only by policy text.
3. **DeviceCheck.** 2 bits, per device, by design, in the docs.
4. **Careless Whisper.** Session-count, session creation order, per-session OS, and per-session liveness — from a phone number, silently, at sub-second sampling, on WhatsApp and Signal. RAID 2025 Best Paper.
5. **Cross-browser fingerprinting at 83.24%** (Cao NDSS 2017), fingerprint-drift re-linking at 99.1%/0.86% FP (Eckersley 2010).
6. **Cross-app identifier reconstitution** post-IDFA: 9 apps, Umeng AAID, server-side (Kollnig FAccT 2022).
7. **Flow correlation at 96%** on 900 packets, retrain monthly (DeepCorr CCS 2018); **hidden-service usage detection at >98% TPR / <0.1% FPR** (Kwon USENIX 2015); **onion-service guard capture at >90% for USD 11,000 over 8 months** (Biryukov S&P 2013).

**The defender's genuine cover, also measured:**
- Website fingerprinting **does not scale** at Internet scale (Panchenko NDSS 2016) — "cannot be reliably used to convict users, but it may be used to limit the set of possible suspects."
- WF accuracy falls **under 50% within 10 days** and **~68%** under multi-tab (Juarez CCS 2014).
- APNs tokens are genuinely per-app-per-device; App Attest keys are per-account-per-device *by Apple's own recommendation*.
- Google abandoned Web Environment Integrity outright (Nov 2023), and its explainer conceded the cross-site linkage risk in writing.

**Real gaps — nobody has measured these:**
- Attestation (Play Integrity / App Attest / DeviceCheck) as a *fingerprinting* channel. Zero arXiv hits for attestation+privacy+linkability. The risk is documented by the vendors and unmeasured by researchers.
- IP-based cross-device/household-graph *accuracy*. The FTC 2017 report describes the mechanism and gives no match rate. The graphs are proprietary.
- Backup metadata as a cross-persona linkage vector. Obvious mechanism, no study.
- **Passive linkage of N pseudonyms on one device by correlated activity timing.** The UIL literature is graph/content/GPS-based, mostly reports only relative improvements, and does not address this. Careless Whisper gets there actively instead.

**Marked NOT REACHED:** Reuters, AP, Wired, The Verge, Washington Post, Axios (403), EFF Deeplinks (404), Google Transparency Report and all support.google.com (CAPTCHA), Google's Android backup E2EE primary text, FP-Stalker full paper (HAL/IEEE/author-site all blocked — its numbers above come from the S&P 2018 program page and should be re-verified), onlinestatusmonitor.com 2014 data summary. No 2024 or 2025 Wyden push-notification follow-up exists on wyden.senate.gov — searched three result pages; that is a negative finding, not a fetch failure.
